<https://beatscript.app/ontology/concept/form-fugue> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept> .
<https://beatscript.app/ontology/concept/form-fugue> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://beatscript.app/ontology/ns#Form> .
<https://beatscript.app/ontology/concept/form-fugue> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <https://beatscript.app/docs/forms#fugue> .
<https://beatscript.app/ontology/concept/form-fugue> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The fugue, a form from classical music: subject answer counter sung answer counter sung: the voices enter one by one. The subject re-enters transposed (`~ subject_* down 2`, later `~ up 2`) while the countersubject morphs toward it (`~ counter_* morph subject_1 p`)."@en .
<https://beatscript.app/ontology/concept/form-fugue> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "fugue"@en .
<https://beatscript.app/ontology/concept/form-fugue> <https://beatscript.app/ontology/ns#formShape> "subject answer counter sung answer counter sung: the voices enter one by one"@en .
<https://beatscript.app/ontology/concept/form-fugue> <https://beatscript.app/ontology/ns#formTemplate> <https://beatscript.app/ontology/concept/template-fugue> .
<https://beatscript.app/ontology/concept/form-fugue> <https://beatscript.app/ontology/ns#mutationPlan> "the subject re-enters transposed (`~ subject_* down 2`, later `~ up 2`) while the countersubject morphs toward it (`~ counter_* morph subject_1 p`)"@en .
